WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.832
Welcome to domain four of the Network Plus N10-009 exam: network security.

00:00:04.832 --> 00:00:07.509
It is worth fourteen percent of the exam.

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:14.234
We will cover basic security concepts, the attacks you must recognize, and the defenses that stop them.

00:00:14.934 --> 00:00:19.487
Start with the C I A triad: confidentiality, integrity, and availability.

00:00:19.487 --> 00:00:23.229
Least privilege means giving only the access that is needed.

00:00:23.229 --> 00:00:28.405
Defense in depth means layering controls so one failure does not expose everything.

00:00:28.405 --> 00:00:30.963
Zero trust means verifying every request.

00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:38.197
Segmentation, including a screened subnet, also called a D M Z, keeps public servers away from the internal network.

00:00:38.197 --> 00:00:48.301
And know the terms: a threat is something that can cause harm, a vulnerability is a weakness, an exploit uses the weakness, and risk is the likelihood and impact.

00:00:49.001 --> 00:00:53.930
For authentication, triple A means authentication, authorization, and accounting.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:01:00.136
M F A uses two or more factors, such as something you know, something you have, and something you are.

00:01:00.136 --> 00:01:05.065
S S O lets one login cover many services, and sam el is a common standard for it.

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:09.446
Radius and tack axe plus provide central triple A for devices and users.

00:01:09.446 --> 00:01:12.853
Kerberos uses tickets, and L D A P looks up a directory.

00:01:12.853 --> 00:01:19.425
Eight oh two dot one X and N A C make a device authenticate before its port or wireless link becomes usable.

00:01:19.425 --> 00:01:22.832
And P K I provides certificates and trusted authorities.

00:01:23.532 --> 00:01:25.873
Some environments need special care.

00:01:25.873 --> 00:01:29.905
Geofencing allows or blocks access based on physical location.

00:01:29.905 --> 00:01:36.668
S C A D A, I C S, and O T systems control industrial equipment, and they should be on isolated segments.

00:01:36.668 --> 00:01:40.960
The same goes for I o T and legacy devices that cannot be patched.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:44.732
And time-based authentication limits when access is valid.

00:01:45.432 --> 00:01:46.138
Now attacks.

00:01:46.138 --> 00:01:52.729
A denial of service attack floods a target until it cannot serve users, and a distributed one uses many sources.

00:01:52.729 --> 00:01:56.318
A R P poisoning sends fake A R P replies to redirect traffic.

00:01:56.318 --> 00:02:00.320
D N S poisoning plants bad D N S data so users reach the wrong site.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.027
M A C flooding fills the switch table so the switch floods frames to every port.

00:02:05.027 --> 00:02:10.206
An on-path attack, also called man in the middle, puts the attacker between two parties.

00:02:10.206 --> 00:02:14.678
And deauthentication attacks use forged frames to kick wireless clients off.

00:02:15.378 --> 00:02:16.148
More attacks.

00:02:16.148 --> 00:02:19.940
A rogue D H C P server hands out bad gateway and D N S settings.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.621
A rogue access point is an unauthorized access point plugged into your network.

00:02:24.621 --> 00:02:28.354
An evil twin is a fake access point that copies a real S S I D.

00:02:28.354 --> 00:02:32.798
V LAN hopping uses double tagging or switch spoofing to reach other V LANs.

00:02:32.798 --> 00:02:38.841
Phishing tricks a person into revealing credentials, and ransomware encrypts data and demands payment.

00:02:39.541 --> 00:02:42.225
Objective four point three is defenses.

00:02:42.225 --> 00:02:44.496
Match each attack to its defense.

00:02:44.496 --> 00:02:46.905
Port security stops M A C flooding.

00:02:46.905 --> 00:02:50.002
D H C P snooping stops rogue D H C P servers.

00:02:50.002 --> 00:02:53.236
Dynamic A R P inspection stops A R P poisoning.

00:02:53.236 --> 00:02:55.645
B P D U guard stops a rogue switch.

00:02:55.645 --> 00:03:01.426
To stop V LAN hopping, disable trunking on access ports and change the native V LAN.

00:03:01.426 --> 00:03:03.284
And shut down unused ports.

00:03:03.984 --> 00:03:05.033
Network defenses.

00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:11.144
Access control lists and firewall rules permit or deny traffic, and they end with an implicit deny.

00:03:11.144 --> 00:03:14.601
An I D S detects attacks, and an I P S also blocks them.

00:03:14.601 --> 00:03:18.181
U R L and content filtering blocks categories of websites.

00:03:18.181 --> 00:03:21.761
A honeypot is a decoy that attracts and studies attackers.

00:03:21.761 --> 00:03:24.786
Segmentation limits how far an attack can spread.

00:03:24.786 --> 00:03:29.909
Change default passwords on every device, and patch to close known vulnerabilities.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:38.280
For wireless and remote access, use W P A three, or W P A two with A E S, and never W E P or an open network.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:42.503
Use eight oh two dot one X with radius for enterprise Wi-Fi.

00:03:42.503 --> 00:03:44.896
Disable unused services and ports.

00:03:44.896 --> 00:03:48.555
Encrypt remote access with S S H, a V P N, or T L S.

00:03:48.555 --> 00:03:51.793
Log and monitor, and actually review the logs.

00:03:51.793 --> 00:03:56.789
And apply a secure baseline to every device, which is called hardening.

00:03:57.489 --> 00:04:03.430
To recap domain four: know the C I A triad, least privilege, zero trust, and triple A.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:11.097
Match each attack to its defense: port security, D H C P snooping, dynamic A R P inspection, and B P D U guard.

00:04:11.097 --> 00:04:14.757
Remember that access lists end with an implicit deny.

00:04:14.757 --> 00:04:18.626
And always change defaults, patch, segment, and monitor.

00:04:18.626 --> 00:04:19.316
Good luck.
